muros (0.9.67) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Boot: the boot lock and the syshook levels are back. Removing /etc/rc left
    nobody holding the exclusive lock on /var/run/booting, so product::booting()
    was always false: exit_on_bootup() deferred nothing during the boot chain and
    the boot performance log stayed empty. Nothing ran the early and start syshook
    levels either, so the templates were not regenerated before the chain and the
    deferred work of the start level (dynamic addresses acquired during the chain,
    syslog and cron restarts, tunables of late loaded modules) never happened. The
    boot unit now runs the PHP subr, the early level, the chain under flock, the
    start level, and the stop level on shutdown, which until now only an operator
    reboot from the interface triggered.
  * Boot: the early syshooks are Debian. 05-upgrade installed the kernel, base and
    package sets staged by opnsense-update, which does not exist here; apt applies
    everything before the reboot, so the hook finishes an interrupted dpkg run
    instead. 10-configd starts the unit rather than the rc script. The rc scripts
    of configd and flowd_aggregate are gone with them.
  * Boot: the DHCP lease watcher is ordered after the boot chain. Its path and
    timer were pulled in by paths.target and timers.target, long before the
    interfaces are configured, and networkd usually gets its first lease exactly
    there: the handler then replayed the newwanip chain and asked configd for a
    reconfiguration while the chain was still configuring the same interface. The
    handler also checks the boot lock itself for the window the ordering cannot
    cover.
  * System: the boot and shutdown melodies play again. opnsense-beep tested
    /dev/speaker and ran the FreeBSD beep port with a pitch in units of 1/100 s;
    it now uses beep(1), which drives the PC speaker on Linux and takes hertz and
    milliseconds. The melody files keep their units. The binary is a suggestion,
    a machine without a PC speaker exits silently.
  * Templates: the generated /etc/rc.conf.d fragments of the ported services are
    gone, they were read by rc.freebsd. The netflow template keeps netflow.conf,
    the shaper template disappears entirely since tc_apply.py reads the model,
    and so do the dead shaper scripts (dummynet statistics and its library, the
    ipfw table updater, the ipfw and pf load order fixup, the dnctl setup).

 -- MurOS <jpzi@proton.me>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 08:20:00 +0200

muros (0.9.66) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Boot: the configuration boot chain runs as a unit. On FreeBSD /etc/rc ran
    rc.bootup, which walks the whole configuration (login and resolver setup,
    trust store, timezone, hostname, routing and the plugin chains); nothing
    called it here, so every service described in config.xml stayed down until an
    operator applied a page in the interface. The interactive interface
    assignment it can reach is now conditional on an attached console so it
    cannot hang the boot.
  * Boot: the FreeBSD service and loader scaffolding is gone (/etc/rc with its
    gpart and tunefs filesystem growth, the loader fragments, and rc.freebsd, an
    rcorder walker over the generated rc.conf.d fragments). rc.reload_all and two
    syshooks still called rc.freebsd, so a full reload ended on a missing script
    instead of reloading anything.
  * Traffic shaper: applying the page reloads the queueing disciplines and then
    the ruleset that feeds them, instead of asking configd for the ipfw template
    and rules that no longer exist. The statistics are reported in the shape the
    page reads, with the counters of the classification rules taken from the
    ruleset, which carries the rule uuid in its comment. The shaper service entry
    also points at the tc backend instead of ipfw.

