muros (0.9.62) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Traffic shaper: ported to the Linux queueing disciplines. Shaping was
    dummynet driven by dnctl with ipfw doing the classification, so the whole
    feature was inert on Debian: the reload action started FreeBSD rc scripts
    and the statistics came from a binary that is not installed. A pipe is now an
    HTB class carrying its bandwidth on every interface a rule uses it on, a
    queue a child class weighted inside its pipe, the leaf discipline fq_codel or
    netem when the pipe adds delay, and the counters come from tc. The ruleset
    classifies traffic by setting the packet priority to the handle of the pipe
    or queue a shaper rule targets, which is what HTB dequeues on, including the
    ACK only and non ACK matches used to protect acknowledgements.
  * Recovery: the recover helper restores what MurOS owns on this platform (the
    PHP drop-ins of both SAPIs with their runtime directories and a cleared
    opcode cache, the login shell list, the bogons files, the runtime tree) and
    finishes an interrupted package configuration with dpkg. It used to restore
    /etc/master.passwd, /etc/group and /etc/ttys and rebuild the password
    databases with pwd_mkdb, none of which exists here.
  * systemd: the CARP reconfiguration and the WireGuard endpoint re-resolution
    are started through /usr/bin, so the timer finally runs; nothing creates the
    /usr/local/bin/python3 the unit pointed at.
  * Unbound: the unreachable FreeBSD chroot mount code is gone.

 -- MurOS <dev@muros.org>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 07:40:00 +0200

muros (0.9.61) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Proxy ARP virtual IPs: answered by the kernel through proxy neighbour
    entries instead of choparp, which is not packaged for Debian, so the mode
    silently did nothing. A network is expanded into one entry per address, its
    network and broadcast addresses skipped, anything wider than a /20 refused;
    proxy NDP is enabled on the interface for the IPv6 case and the entries are
    dropped again when the virtual IP goes down.
  * NetFlow: flows are exported by softflowd, one instance per interface and per
    collector. The exporter used to live in the kernel (ng_netflow nodes driven
    by ngctl, datagrams fanned out by samplicate), so the feature could not run
    at all here. The collectors are rendered as host:port, egress only
    interfaces are captured with an outbound filter and the statistics come from
    softflowctl. Local collection still relies on flowd, which has no Debian
    package.
  * Health: the time graph is fed from the chrony tracking line instead of the
    ntpq variables of a daemon this platform does not ship, so the database was
    only ever updated with unknowns.
  * Interfaces: the MAC spoofing helper of the settings page reads the neighbour
    table with iproute2 instead of arp(8), and validates the client address
    before using it.

