muros (0.9.57) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Interfaces: finish the ifconfig removal in interface_configure(). MAC
    spoofing, promiscuous mode, the interface alias and the link up now go
    through ip link, and the per-interface IPv6 behaviour (accept_ra,
    accept_dad, disable_ipv6) through /proc/sys/net/ipv6/conf/<dev>/, with
    accept_ra=2 where advertisements must be accepted since the box forwards.
    The promiscuous state was also compared against the FreeBSD ppromisc flag
    name, which iproute2 never reports.
  * Interfaces: the 6rd and 6to4 tracking interfaces assign and remove their
    delegated /64 with iproute2 instead of ifconfig inet6 prefixlen, so a LAN
    tracking such a WAN finally gets its address.
  * Interfaces: the point to point peer reported by iproute2 is exposed as the
    endpoint field of the address, which the OpenVPN link-up hook needs to find
    the tunnel gateway and the IPsec vti bring-up to match its remote.
  * DHCP client: stop spawning isc dhclient next to systemd-networkd, which owns
    the addressing of every assigned interface. Two clients raced for the
    address, the routes and the resolver, and dhclient is deprecated in Debian
    13. An interface apply re-renders the networkd units and reconfigures the
    link instead, and an interface reset releases the lease the same way.
  * DHCP client: the DHCP options of the interface (sent hostname, fixed alias
    address, honour the offered MTU, servers to reject) are rendered into the
    networkd unit instead of being lost with the dhclient configuration.
  * DHCP client: new muros-lease-watch.path/.service, which replaces the
    dhclient-script hook. networkd has no per-lease hook, so the lease directory
    is watched and the newwanip chain (ifctl router, nameservers and search
    domain, then configd) is replayed for the interfaces whose lease changed. A
    DHCP WAN could otherwise renew or change address without a gateway update,
    a ruleset reload or a dynamic DNS update.
  * OpenVPN: instances run under a muros-openvpn@.service template instead of an
    unsupervised openvpn --config, so a crashed tunnel is restarted and its
    output reaches the journal.
  * Packaging: stage-fs.sh relocates every unit type into the package unit
    directory, not only *.service. The WireGuard re-resolution timer was left in
    /usr/local/etc/systemd/system, where systemd never looks.

 -- MurOS <dev@muros.org>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 21:45:00 +0200

muros (0.9.56) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Policy based routing (route-to/reply-to): finish the port.
    - setup_policy_routing.php was never invoked, so the gateway marks emitted
      by the ruleset landed on packets with no matching routing table or ip
      rule and pinned flows quietly followed the default route. nft_apply.sh now
      reconciles the tables after every ruleset load, with a new --auto mode
      that reads the active gateways (name, address, device) from the Gateways
      model. Best effort: a routing problem cannot fail the firewall reload.
    - The lookup rule is created in the address family of the gateway. An IPv6
      gateway had its table populated with ip -6 but the steering rule created
      with a plain ip rule (IPv4 only), and stale IPv6 rules were never
      reconciled.
    - Model (MVC) firewall rules honour their gateway selection like the legacy
      rules did, and the mangle copy of the match drops an egress interface
      match that prerouting cannot evaluate.
  * Firewall: per-rule logging on legacy filter rules, which emitted no nft log
    statement at all, so those rules were invisible in the log viewer. The
    legacy rule uuid is read from the rule attribute where the edit page stores
    it. Legacy floating rules also resolve a comma separated interface list and
    honour interfacenot; the list used to fail to resolve and silently widened
    the rule to every interface.
  * Firewall states: real age. net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_timestamp is enabled by
    default (visible as a tunable) and the conntrack delta-time is read back, so
    the states view and pftop show an age instead of 0 and pftop can compute a
    bytes per second average.
  * WAN address change: rc.newwanip flushes the stale flows with conntrack on the
    four tuple ends (a source NATed flow only carries the address in its reply
    tuple) and by gateway mark, instead of the pfctl -k calls that did nothing.
  * OpenVPN: instances run as systemd units (muros-openvpn@.service template,
    instantiated on the mode id) instead of an unsupervised openvpn --config, so
    a crashed tunnel is restarted and its output reaches the journal. The pid
    file contract and the GUI service status are unchanged.
  * Interfaces: the iproute2 details parser exposes the point to point peer as
    the endpoint field, which the OpenVPN link-up hook needs to find the tunnel
    gateway and the IPsec vti bring-up to match its remote.
  * HA: conntrackd is only restarted when its generated configuration actually
    changed (a restart drops the state caches, and the generator runs on every
    interface or filter reconfiguration), and a FAULT transition demotes
    conntrackd like a backup transition does.

 -- MurOS <dev@muros.org>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 21:20:00 +0200

muros (0.9.55) trixie; urgency=medium

  * HA (CARP/VRRP): permit incoming VRRP adverts on the firewall input chain.
    - nft_build.php now emits an automatic "meta l4proto 112 accept" rule in
      the input chain whenever at least one CARP virtual IP is configured. The
      default-deny input chain previously dropped the keepalived VRRP adverts
      (IP protocol 112, multicast 224.0.0.18 / ff02::12 or unicast HA), so every
      node stopped hearing its peer and elected itself master (split brain).
      This mirrors the automatic "CARP defaults" pass rule that OPNsense ships;
      the legacy filter.lib.inc generator that built it is not consumed by the
      nft data plane, so the rule had to be ported into nft_build.php directly.
    - reconfigure_vips.php triggers a "filter reload" when CARP VIPs are present
      so adding the first CARP VIP toggles the rule on without a manual reload.
    - Validated end to end on a two node bench: correct master/backup election
      from advskew, VIP ownership on the master, failover to the backup with no
      ping loss when the master is stopped, and preemptive failback on recovery.

 -- MurOS <dev@muros.org>  Sat, 27 Jun 2026 18:55:00 +0200

muros (0.9.54) trixie; urgency=medium

  * IPsec: fix the swanctl updown handler for routed IPsec and manual policies.
    - Route-based (VTI) tunnels no longer run the FreeBSD "ifconfig ipsecN
      reqid/tunnel" sequence on the up event. That binary and syntax do not
      exist on Linux and aborted the event; the ipsec<reqid> XFRM interface is
      already created up front by ipsec_configure_vti() with if_id = reqid and
      charon binds the SAs through if_id_in/if_id_out, so there is nothing to do
      at updown time.
    - The reqid_events.conf configd target was still written to the FreeBSD
      path /usr/local/etc/swanctl while the rest of the IPsec stack (swanctl.conf,
      certificates) and the updown handler use /etc/swanctl. The file is now
      written to /etc/swanctl so manual policies are actually found.
    - Manual security policies (the phase 2 "spd" field) are installed through
      "ip xfrm policy" instead of the FreeBSD/KAME setkey tool, tagged with the
      tunnel reqid and reapplied idempotently on each up event. Verified on
      Debian 13 for IPv4 and IPv6 selectors.

 -- MurOS <dev@muros.org>  Sat, 27 Jun 2026 17:55:00 +0200

muros (0.9.53) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Kea DHCP: disable the packaged AppArmor profiles for the Kea daemons
    (kea-dhcp4, kea-dhcp6, kea-ctrl-agent, kea-lfc) on install and upgrade. The
    stock profiles denied the MurOS-rendered configuration under /etc/kea and
